    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 7 tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The audit log confirms the two household members were created with `membershipLevel=1` and `membershipLevel=2` \u2014 the cap held (level 2 \u2264 1+1).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their existing household members; with no existing member, no cap.\n\n**Change 1 \u2014 the feature (`CapOwnerMembershipLevel`).** I kept `AssignOwnerMembershipLevel` focused solely on the points\u2192level mapping and added a new single-responsibility step `CapOwnerMembershipLevel.java` that runs right after it in the create transaction. It looks up existing owners sharing the built owner's household id, takes their maximum `membershipLevel`, and caps via `min(derivedLevel, maxLevel + 1)`. With no existing household member, `max()` is empty and no cap is applied \u2014 exactly as specified. It reuses the same repository-filter-by-household-id pattern already established by `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize`, and is wired into `owners.POST.yml` between `assignMembershipLevel` and `assignBulkSignupWarning`.\n\n**Change 2 \u2014 an unblocking bug fix (`EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity`).** The acceptance test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName+postcode, different telephone) and requires both to be created so the cap can be exercised. The baseline rejected the second with a 409 because `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` treated *any* same-household owner as a hard duplicate. That contradicted the codebase's own documented design: `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate` handles a same-household **different-telephone** owner as a permitted *soft* duplicate (\"not a hard household duplicate, which is rejected upstream\") \u2014 logic that was unreachable dead code. I narrowed the hard-duplicate check to require the same household **and** the same telephone, which both unblocks the test and makes the soft-duplicate path reachable as intended.\n\nI flagged this second change explicitly because it goes slightly beyond the literal one-line feature description \u2014 it was necessary because the provided test can't pass otherwise, and it aligns with the existing documented intent rather than inventing new behavior.",
